<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Mersal.ClinicalValidation</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Mersal.ClinicalValidation</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    No FrameworkReference and no dependency on HTTP, EF or a service, deliberately. This library is pure
    domain logic with no I/O: it evaluates already-fetched snapshots. Phase 27's step-2 evaluator consumes
    the SAME library, so step 1 and step 2 cannot drift into two implementations of one rule. A dependency
    on a transport or a persistence layer here would be the first step back towards that drift.

    The ONE ProjectReference below is not a relaxation of that rule. Mersal.Ingredients is pure domain
    vocabulary with the same no-I/O constraint — it answers "what molecule is this", which this engine and
    masterdata must answer IDENTICALLY or an interaction rule authored against one ingredient will screen a
    prescription against another. The ban was always on I/O, not on shared meaning; phase 28 makes that
    distinction explicit rather than duplicating the normaliser to preserve a literal reading of it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../ingredients/Mersal.Ingredients.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../clinical-codes/Mersal.ClinicalCodes.csproj" />
    <!--
      Mersal.Prescribing is the same kind of reference and is admitted for the same reason: pure arithmetic
      with no I/O, and one answer that two callers must not compute differently. `QuantityMath` decides how
      much medicine is handed over; the composer fills the field in from it and this engine grades it. Two
      implementations of that division is two chances to hand a patient the wrong amount.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../prescribing/Mersal.Prescribing.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Remove="Tests/**/*.cs" />
    <None Remove="Tests/**/*" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
